.TH DDCOLORD.TOML 5 2026-11-27 "ddcolord 0.1.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
ddcolord.toml \- ddcolord configuration
.SH DESCRIPTION
A TOML file mapping monitors, and the values of their color mode feature, to
ICC profiles.
Unknown keys are an error, so a misspelt key fails to load instead of being
ignored.
Every ICC file is read at load time and must be a whole ICC profile: the
ICC signature, and a size and tag table that fit in the file.
A missing file, one that is not an ICC profile, or a truncated copy is an
error then, not at the first mode switch.
.SH TOP LEVEL
.TP
.BR interval " (integer, default 3)"
Seconds between polls.
At least 1.
.TP
.BR notify " (boolean, default false)"
Show a desktop notification, through the notification daemon on the
session bus, each time a mode switch changes the profile.
The summary names the monitor and the mode, the body the profile's file
and the connector; the next switch replaces it.
The first profile after start, or after a monitor appears, is not
announced, nor is a mode that keeps the current profile.
Without a notification daemon, each switch logs a warning and the profile
still changes.
.TP
.B [[monitor]]
One table per monitor model.
The first entry that matches an output's EDID wins.
.SH MONITOR
.TP
.BR vendor " (string, required)"
EDID three-letter PNP manufacturer ID, for example
.BR BNQ .
.TP
.BR model " (string, required)"
EDID monitor name descriptor, for example
.BR "BenQ PD3220U" .
A monitor whose EDID has no name descriptor cannot be matched.
.TP
.BR serial " (string)"
EDID serial string, not empty.
Only needed to give two identical monitors different tables; when unset,
any serial matches.
.TP
.BR feature " (integer, default 0xdc)"
VCP feature code that reports the color mode.
MCCS calls 0xDC "Display Mode"; vendors fill its values with their own
mode numbers.
.TP
.BR unmapped " (path)"
Profile for a mode that has no
.B icc
of its own, and for a value not listed at all.
When unset, such a mode leaves the current profile in place.
.TP
.B [[monitor.mode]]
One table per mode.
.SH MODE
.TP
.BR code " (integer, required)"
Low byte (SL) of the feature's value.
.TP
.BR sub " (integer)"
High byte (SH).
An entry with
.B sub
matches only that value; one without matches any.
The exact match wins when both exist.
.TP
.BR name " (string, required)"
Used in logs and by
.BR "ddcolord \-\-probe" .
.TP
.BR icc " (path)"
ICC profile for this mode.
A relative path is relative to the config file's directory.
.SH NOTES
Monitors may answer a VCP code they do not implement with the values of
their previous reply rather than an error, and some (the BenQ PD3220U among
them) label that copy with the requested code, so it cannot be told from a
real reply.
Set
.B feature
only to a code the monitor declares in its capabilities string
.RB ( "ddcutil capabilities" ).
.PP
Generic CC0 profiles for sRGB, Rec.709, Adobe RGB (1998), Display P3 and
DCI\-P3 come with ddcolord: copied into
.I icc/
beside a starter config, and installed in
.IR <prefix>/share/ddcolord/examples/icc/ .
Their README says what each assumes.
A profile measured for the monitor itself is always the better choice.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
interval = 3

[[monitor]]
vendor = "BNQ"
model = "BenQ PD3220U"
unmapped = "icc/sRGB-v4.icc"

[[monitor.mode]]
code = 0x0a
name = "sRGB"
icc = "icc/sRGB-v4.icc"

[[monitor.mode]]
code = 0x25
name = "Adobe RGB"
icc = "icc/AdobeCompat-v4.icc"

[[monitor.mode]]
code = 0x13
sub = 0x04
name = "Low Blue Light: Reading"
.fi
.PP
A complete table for the BenQ PD3220U is installed as
.IR <prefix>/share/ddcolord/examples/benq\-pd3220u.toml ,
with its profiles in
.I icc/
beside it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ddcolord (1)
